# Kenya Campus Hub / Site Chat — connected & fixed

## What was actually wrong

`server.js` already had a huge, well-built Socket.IO chat backend (presence,
1:1 + group chat, calls, reactions, pins, disappearing messages, friend
requests, invite links...). But `client.js` (the "Site Chat" UI in
`nodi.html`) had fully-built front-end features whose events the server
**never listened for**, so those buttons quietly did nothing:

| Client feature              | Event(s)                                                            | Status before |
|------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------|
| Block contact                | `block-user`                                                          | no server handler |
| Clear chat                   | `clear-chat`                                                           | no server handler |
| Status / Stories             | `get-statuses`, `post-status`, `view-status`, `delete-status`         | no server handler |
| Channels                     | `get-channels`, `create-channel`                                      | no server handler |
| Communities                  | `get-communities`, `create-community`                                 | no server handler |
| Star a message (context menu)| `message-context-action`                                              | no server handler |

I added all six to `server.js`, in the same style as the existing code
(best-effort JSON files under the OS temp dir — same approach already used
for chat history, groups, and the users registry). I also wired **block**
into the existing `chat-message` handler so a block actually stops messages
in both directions, not just the button.

I verified every event `client.js` emits now has a matching `server.js`
listener, and every event `server.js` emits has a matching `client.js`
listener — so the whole socket surface is fully connected in both
directions.

One honest limitation: this server has no persistent "who's friends with
whom" list server-side (that already lived only in each browser's
localStorage, by the original design). So Status privacy options ("My
contacts" / "Only share with...") are stored but not enforced — every
signed-in user can currently see every status. Doing that properly needs a
real server-side contacts list, which is a bigger change than a wiring fix.

## What I could *not* verify or include

Only `server.js`, `client.js`, `nodi.html`, and `nodi.css` were uploaded.
`server.js` also serves `public/index.html`, `public/article.html`, and an
articles/news feature (`data/articles.json`, `ai-updater.js`) — none of
those files were provided, so I added tiny placeholder `index.html` /
`article.html` pages (linking through to the chat) just so those two routes
don't 500. Drop your real versions into `public/` to replace them.

I also don't have network access in this environment, so I could not run
`npm install` or actually boot the server to test it live — everything
above was verified by static analysis (`node --check` passes, and every
socket event was cross-referenced by name between client and server). Run
it locally to confirm before deploying.

## Running it

```bash
npm install
cp .env.example .env    # optional - chat works with none of these set
npm start
```

Then open **http://localhost:3000/nodi.html** — that's the chat app. Open it
in two browser tabs/windows with two different names to test 1:1 chat,
calls, groups, statuses, etc.

`GROQ_API_KEY` / `NEWSDATA_API_KEY` / `GIPHY_API_KEY` are all optional — the
chat feature works fully without them; they only power the AI assistant and
news-related endpoints elsewhere in `server.js`.